<Base title="About — What It Takes to Win">
  <section class="hero" style="padding-bottom:24px;">
    <h1>About this project</h1>
    <p class="lede">An open visualization of an early-advantage dataset covering 2,585 people.</p>
  </section>

  <article class="prose">
    <h2>What this is</h2>
    <p>
      A research beta mapping people who reached a material professional, commercial, creative,
      athletic, or research milestone early in their careers — and the early conditions that may have
      accelerated their development: family resources, relevant parents, rare tools, elite
      institutions, frontier ecosystems, mentors, peers, platforms, direct domain exposure,
      unusual ability, and adversity.
    </p>
    <p>
      The product reads each record as a four-stage path: starting conditions, converted leverage,
      compounding trajectory, and observed career standing. The early milestone determines inclusion;
      the tier summarizes documented career recognition through the data cutoff. Its central finding is
      a gradient rather than a formula: higher tiers have higher average advantage and leverage, but
      their score ranges overlap substantially. See <a href="/insights/">how the model and tiers work</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What it is not</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Not a census or representative sample of successful people.</li>
      <li>Not a causal estimate of which advantages produce success.</li>
      <li>Not a ranking of human worth or talent.</li>
      <li>Not a predictor of individual outcomes.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Two evidence layers</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Biographical layer</strong> — names, milestones, age assignments, early-history
      summaries, family context, and source URLs — intended to summarize public biographical
      evidence.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Annotation layer</strong> — leverage engines and numeric scores — are analyst
      interpretations applied through a common rubric. They are hypotheses about acceleration
      mechanisms, not measurements.
    </p>

    <h2>Source & release terms</h2>
    <p>
      This research build includes 2,585 people born in or after 1950. Release terms for the
      original annotations and scripts are still under review; the repository does not yet grant
      the suggested CC BY 4.0 or MIT licences. Third-party sources retain their own terms.
      See <a href="https://github.com/sarthakagrawal927/success-by-26">GitHub</a> for source.
    </p>

    <h2>Building the site locally</h2>
    <p>
      <code>npm install &amp;&amp; npm run dev</code> — Astro dev server on
      <code>http://localhost:4321</code>. Build with <code>npm run build</code>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
